# people-agent core

## Repository identity

- This is the standalone VS Archive `people-agent`.
- Main application repository: `/home/yshif/vs-archive/vs-archive`.
- Historical/legacy people-agent workspace: `/mnt/c/Users/User/vs-archive-people-agent`.
- This repository is greenfield. Do not copy the historical pipeline wholesale.

## Repository boundary

- Do not import Django or main-repository application modules into this package.
- Do not connect directly to the production database.
- Do not access or mutate AWS/production infrastructure.
- Do not infer production state from a local database.
- Integration with `vs-archive` happens through explicit versioned artifacts.

## Human-review boundary

Human review remains mandatory for new Person creation, ambiguous identity, merge decisions, canonical-name changes, inferred aliases, biography decisions, and production writes.

A deterministic production identity match may reduce review work. It is not itself a production write or apply operation.

identity-review-package-v2 keeps every classification, including DETERMINISTIC_EXISTING_PERSON, in the human-review queue.

## Git/workflow

- Never commit directly to `main`.
- Every task starts on a dedicated branch.
- Do not use `git add .`.
- Do not commit or push unless the user explicitly asks.
- Do not merge to `main` without the branch validation and review workflow.
- Do not commit generated files or build metadata.

## Execution policy

- Do not mutate production, AWS, or provider state without explicit approval.
- Do not run apply, deploy, or production commands without explicit approval.
- Tests, Ruff, and formatter checks are quality gates before a branch is ready.
- If the user reserves those commands, do not run them unless asked.

## Engineering expectations

- Prefer a principled architectural fix over a patch or workaround.
- Preserve deterministic behavior.
- Fail closed when a contract or lineage is invalid.
- Do not silently reinterpret or upgrade a versioned artifact.
- Do not change a versioned contract casually. A schema-breaking change needs an explicit new version.
- Do not broaden task scope unnecessarily.

